      "Candomblé's cosmology centers on axé (ashé), a Yoruba-derived concept understood as vital spiritual force, divine energy, and the power to make things happen. Axé flows from Olodumare (Olorun, Olofin)—the supreme creator deity, distant and impersonal—through the deities (orixás in Ketu, voduns in Jeje, inkices in Angola, collectively referred to as 'saints' or santos) to humans, plants, animals, minerals, and natural forces. Axé is not static but circulates and accumulates through ritual action: sacrifice feeds axé to deities, offerings channel it, initiations transmit it, and correct behavior (including moral conduct, respect for elders, and ritual purity) preserves it. A person's well-being, health, prosperity, and spiritual development depend on maintaining balanced axé.",
      "The deities are not abstract principles but personified forces governing natural domains and human experiences. In Ketu-Nagô Candomblé, major orixás include: Exu (guardian of crossroads, communication, and cosmic order, often misidentified with the Christian devil); Ogum (iron, war, technology, labor); Oxóssi (hunting, forests, sustenance); Obaluaiyê/Omolu (disease, healing, earth); Xangô (thunder, fire, justice, kingship); Oxum (rivers, love, fertility, wealth, beauty); Iemanjá (ocean, motherhood, nurturing); Iansã/Oyá (wind, storms, cemeteries, transformation); Oxumarê (rainbow, continuity, cycles); Nanã (primordial earth, death, ancestral wisdom); Oxalá (creation, purity, peace, wisdom, eldest deity). Each orixá possesses distinct colors, foods, songs, drum rhythms, dance movements, sacred plants, and moral attributes. Practitioners do not worship all orixás equally; through divination at initiation, each person learns which orixá 'owns their head' (dono da cabeça), establishing a lifelong tutelary relationship.",
