      "Candomblé posits a complex relationship between destiny, free will, and spirit possession. Before birth, each soul negotiates its destiny (enredo, caminho) with Olodumare, choosing life circumstances, challenges, and the orixá who will accompany them. Upon incarnation, this pre-birth choice is forgotten but can be revealed through divination (jogo de búzios, cowrie shell casting). Destiny is not deterministic—humans possess agency to improve or worsen their path through ritual intervention (ebós, offerings), moral conduct, and alignment with their orixá. Spirit possession (transe, santo feito) is the central ritual mechanism: when an orixá 'mounts' (monta) a devotee during drumming ceremonies, the possessed individual loses ordinary consciousness and embodies the deity, who dances, gestures, speaks, and interacts with the community. Possession demonstrates the deity's presence and favor, provides oracular guidance, and renews axé.",
