      "Candomblé practice centers on the terreiro, a sacred compound that is simultaneously a temple, community center, and initiatory space. Terreiros vary in size and elaboration—from modest urban houses with a single ritual room to expansive rural estates with multiple shrines, sacred groves (matos), and residential structures. Each terreiro is led by a mãe or pai-de-santo, supported by a hierarchy of initiates based on years since initiation (tempo de santo): iaôs (novice initiates, first 7 years), ebomis (senior initiates after completing the 7-year obrigação), and ogãs/ekedes (non-possessing ritual functionaries—ogãs are men who play drums or perform administrative roles; ekedes are women who care for possessed devotees). This gerontocratic structure mirrors West African age-grade systems, adapted to Brazilian contexts.",
      "Divination (consulta or jogo de búzios) is the most frequent ritual interaction, through which practitioners diagnose spiritual problems, identify responsible orixás, and receive prescriptions for restoration. The mãe or pai-de-santo casts sixteen cowrie shells on a mat or divining board, interprets the resulting pattern (odu), tells relevant itans, and prescribes ebós—offerings ranging from simple (lighting candles, herbal baths, feeding one's orixá with honey, fruit, or flowers) to complex (animal sacrifice, elaborate ceremonies requiring multiple days and community participation). Clients pay for divination, though fees vary by region, terreiro, and relationship—godchildren may receive free or discounted consultations. Divination is not fortune-telling but diagnostic spiritual medicine, identifying imbalances in axé and recommending interventions.",
      "Animal sacrifice (matança or sacrifício) is central and controversial. Candomblé views sacrifice as feeding the orixás, releasing axé from the animal into the spiritual realm, and creating reciprocal exchange between humans and deities. The animals—chickens, roosters, guinea fowl, goats, sheep, doves—are consecrated through prayers, ritually killed with prayers invoking the orixá, and their blood offered to sacred stones (otás or pedras de santo) housed in shrines. The meat is cooked and shared communally, with portions for the orixá (placed on altars or buried), the priests, and participants. Sacrifice is understood as more humane and spiritually meaningful than industrial slaughter. However, it faces legal challenges (Brazilian animal welfare laws are ambiguous), evangelical Christian condemnation, and misunderstanding by outsiders who associate it with 'black magic.' Candomblé communities defend sacrifice as constitutionally protected religious freedom.",
      "Initiation (feitura de santo, making the saint) transforms an abian (candidate) into an iaô (initiated devotee of an orixá). The process, lasting 7–21 days depending on nação and terreiro, involves: seclusion (recolhimento) in a ritual room (roncó); head-shaving (raspagem da cabeça); ritual baths with sacred herbs (amacis); painting the body with sacred designs (pinturas); animal sacrifices to 'feed' the orixá and prepare the initiate's ori (head/spirit); possession by the orixá under controlled circumstances; and public presentation (saída de iaô) where the newly initiated dances in elaborate ritual garments. The initiate's head is 'made' (feito)—the orixá is 'seated' (assentado) there, establishing permanent spiritual connection. Post-initiation, the iaô must observe strict taboos for one year (resguardo or preceito): wearing white, avoiding certain foods, not shaking hands, not looking in mirrors, maintaining sexual abstinence. This liminal period marks ritual rebirth.",
